## Project State Summary

**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that re-implements GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities in Go, verifying parity via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation pipeline.

**Architecture:** A four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) produces Go binaries in `cmd/`, supported by shared libraries (`pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `pkg/testutils`) and driven by mage build targets. Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites) are the source of truth; code is generated from them.

**Current state:** 17 commands implemented across generation runs 1–17 (tee, head, seq, echo, tac, nl, fold, expand, unexpand, cut, paste, uniq, comm, md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum). All spec_complete releases through rel05.4 are done. Code lives at generation-merged tags.

**Current release scope:** rel99.2 — the deferred `ts` utility. One use case remains: `rel99.2-uc001-ts` (spec_complete, code not started).

## Priority Reasoning

Release 99.2 is the only release in scope. It contains a single use case for `ts` (moreutils timestamp prepender). The specs are complete; the utility needs implementation. `ts` depends on `pkg/sys` (SIGPIPE handling) and `pkg/testutils` (differential testing), both already implemented. Per P2, implementation and tests are split — with a 1-task limit, the implementation task comes first (P8: implementation before tests).

```yaml
- index: 0
  title: "Implement cmd/ts -- prd-ts core timestamping (R1-R4)"
  dependency: -1
  description: |
    deliverable_type: code

    required_reading:
      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (find and read the ts PRD, likely prd004-ts.yaml or prd005-ts.yaml)
      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel99.2-uc001-ts.yaml (use case for ts)
      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-99.2.yaml (test suite for acceptance reference)
      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section -- differential testing pattern, SIGPIPE handling pattern, graceful skip pattern)
      - pkg/sys/ (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage)
      - pkg/testutils/ (DiffTest, RunDiffTests, BuildBinary contracts)
      - cmd/tee/main.go (reference implementation pattern for a stdin-to-stdout streaming utility)
      - cmd/head/main.go (reference implementation pattern for line-by-line processing)

    files:
      - path: cmd/ts/main.go
        action: create
        note: "ts utility implementation: reads stdin, prepends timestamps, writes to stdout"
      - path: cmd/ts/ts_test.go
        action: create
        note: "Differential tests against moreutils ts reference binary"

    requirements:
      - id: R1
        text: |
          Implement the ts command that reads lines from stdin and prepends a
          formatted timestamp to each line before writing to stdout. The default
          format is the strftime format specified in the ts PRD. Support a custom
          strftime format string as a positional argument. Implement all PRD
          requirements for basic timestamping (read the PRD to identify the exact
          requirement IDs and their details).
      - id: R2
        text: |
          Implement the -s flag for relative (elapsed) timestamps showing time
          since ts started, and the -i flag for incremental timestamps showing
          time since the previous line was output. Implement the -m flag for
          monotonic clock source. Implement all flag interactions as specified
          in the PRD.
      - id: R3
        text: |
          Implement sub-second precision via %.s (epoch with fractional seconds)
          and %.S (seconds with fractional part) or equivalent format extensions
          as specified in the PRD. Handle all format string edge cases documented
          in the PRD.
      - id: R4
        text: |
          Write differential tests in ts_test.go following the project shared
          protocol: use testutils.BuildBinary to compile cmd/ts, use
          exec.LookPath("ts") to find the moreutils reference binary, call
          t.Skip if not found, and run testutils.RunDiffTests with a
          []testutils.DiffTest slice covering all flags and format variations.
          The ts reference binary is "ts" (not "gts" -- moreutils does not use
          g-prefix). Use TimestampNormalizer or a custom NormalizeFunc to handle
          timestamp value differences between runs.

    design_decisions:
      - id: D1
        text: |
          Call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main() per the
          project shared protocol for all cmd/ utilities that write to stdout.
      - id: D2
        text: |
          Use Go's time.Now() for wall clock and time.Now() difference for
          relative/incremental modes. When -m is specified, use a monotonic
          clock source. Go's time package preserves monotonic readings by
          default on time.Now(); use Sub() for elapsed calculations.
      - id: D3
        text: |
          Implement strftime formatting by translating strftime directives to
          Go time.Format layout strings, since Go does not have a native
          strftime function. Handle the %.s and %.S extensions separately
          as string replacements before or after the main format pass.
      - id: D4
        text: |
          For differential tests, timestamps will differ between the Go and
          reference binary runs. Use testutils.TimestampNormalizer or write a
          custom NormalizeFunc that replaces timestamp prefixes with a fixed
          placeholder so stdout comparison succeeds. Alternatively, use format
          strings that produce deterministic output (e.g., fixed epoch) where
          possible, and normalizers where not.

    acceptance_criteria:
      - id: AC1
        text: "cmd/ts/main.go compiles without errors: go build ./cmd/ts/ succeeds"
      - id: AC2
        text: "ts reads stdin line by line and prepends a timestamp in the default format when no arguments are given"
      - id: AC3
        text: "ts accepts a custom strftime format string as a positional argument and formats timestamps accordingly"
      - id: AC4
        text: "The -s flag produces relative timestamps (elapsed since start) and -i produces incremental timestamps (elapsed since previous line)"
      - id: AC5
        text: "The -m flag switches to monotonic clock source"
      - id: AC6
        text: "Sub-second precision works via %.s and %.S format extensions"
      - id: AC7
        text: "Differential tests in ts_test.go pass when the moreutils ts binary is available, and skip gracefully when it is not"
      - id: AC8
        text: "go vet ./cmd/ts/ reports no issues"
```